while, for and ranges in Rust: why 0..5 stops at 4, and why for is safer than a while loop with an index. Rust Day 13 of the Zero to Pro series.

📌 In this short:
✅ while runs as long as its condition is true (a countdown: 3! 2! 1! LIFTOFF!)
✅ for x in a walks through every item, with no index to get wrong
✅ Ranges: 0..5 is 0 to 4, 0..=5 includes 5, and (1..4).rev() counts down 3, 2, 1

💻 Code from the video:
let a = [10, 20, 30];
for x in a {
    println!("{x}");
}
for n in (1..4).rev() {
    println!("{n}!");
}

📖 Learn more: https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch03-05-control-flow.html
